Python List

A Python list is a built-in data structure.

numbers = [10, 20, 30]
Features
- Dynamic size
- Can store different data types
- Supports indexing
- Supports slicing
- Easy to use

Example:
data = [10, "Python", 3.14, True]
print(data) #[10, 'Python', 3.14, True]

Python Array: Python provides an array module(All elements are the same type and stored efficiently)
from array import array
numbers = array('i', [10, 20, 30]) # Here: 'i' means integer array
Features:
- Dynamic size
- Only one data type
- More memory efficient than a list for large collections of numbers
- Faster for numeric data in many cases

 Operation  List              Array (array.array) 
 Indexing  | O(1)           | O(1)                  
 Append    | O(1) amortized | O(1) amortized        
 Insert    | O(n)           | O(n)                  
 Delete    | O(n)           | O(n)                  

Python lists are implemented as dynamic arrays. They provide array-like O(1) indexing and automatic resizing, but unlike traditional arrays, they can store references to objects of different types
-> Python also provides array.array for homogeneous data and NumPy arrays for high-performance numerical computing

 Operation                        Complexity      Why                               
 Access                         | O(1)           | Direct index calculation          
 Update                         | O(1)           | Replace existing value            
 Traverse                       | O(n)           | Visit every element               
 Search (unsorted)              | O(n)           | No ordering information           
 Search (sorted, binary search) | O(log n)       | Eliminate half each step          
 Insert at end (dynamic array)  | Amortized O(1) | Usually append; occasional resize 
 Insert at front                | O(n)           | Shift all elements                
 Delete from middle             | O(n)           | Shift remaining elements          
